      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "Great question! D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is one of the most fascinating and mysterious experiences the brain can produce. Here's a breakdown of what scientists currently understand about it:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde0 What Is D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu?\n\n",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu describes the feeling that something you're currently experiencing has already happened in the past. ",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDIbaW6N4U+CdEWynlxoMWpCEJHofkM0Pp4aoIjB6uxGZxfW2ZY4N4y8fj4F2pxgchyRfjHy84LFJ8Y3M2IAKH/Bntk+ooVqbIFdviaUqE9gZpswDFYkpivW2GVQQa+0b1dAYBA==",
            "title": "What is the science behind d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu? | Live Science",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.livescience.com/health/psychology/what-is-the-science-behind-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu describes the feeling that something you're currently experiencing has already happened in the past.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": " ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Most people experience this sensation, known as d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu, at some point in their lives. It\u2019s a hard feeling to study, though, because it tends to arise...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDElLW0E3c0F1LYBLcRoMJ2jt0yC4xvtLJbNSIjAwNYDivc4/RZ1ptX5RUAitVgGh/vl3UrrgOSGa52GpHONbbPwssA9FwJOYNoNF+bcqFdGufTI7ZBQey8N6iHdps1G1NzouvBgE",
            "title": "What Causes D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu? | Scientific American",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/what-causes-the-feeling-of-deja-vu/"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Most people experience this sensation at some point in their lives, though it's hard to study because it tends to arise spontaneously and be shaken off easily.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f What's Actually Happening in the Brain?\n\n",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "\u201cD\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dysfunctional connections between the parts of your brain that play a role in memory recollection and familiarity,\u201d Dr. Khoury e...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDFl53WV0cD4R88rZFRoMm5TnIWZja3mlNICIIjC0zXNRzR2CbzxnCxk46BUdTrORl0eZ4ImZ5Pb9idruX9TCuv+tUhrl6KORVFlx2scqFVYbkjzkKDRaekQyGEmJcbwm/4Wu3RgE",
            "title": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu: What It Is and Why It Happens",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://health.clevelandclinic.org/deja-vu-what-it-is-and-when-it-may-be-cause-for-concern"
          }
        ],
        "text": "\"D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dysfunctional connections between the parts of your brain that play a role in memory recollection and familiarity. It's a misfire that blurs the line between memory and new experience.\"",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\nMore specifically, ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "According to O'Connor, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas, such as the temporal lobe, send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating tha...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpMBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDImt4rqqmm7EWe58xRoMdel2lc+y+tPnkwktIjBVwA2H6q228Y4fTD/6urRduT/Bg4QsJ2lMO6gMAmn95DtTI2Rc2UR7xNYXsz75SnwqF9pWowX42KopYeMzgfi2YZ/ElYqBPAS9GAQ=",
            "title": "The Fascinating Science of D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu | Psychology Today",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/brain-reboot/202312/the-fascinating-science-of-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas such as the temporal lobe send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating that an experience is repeating itself. The frontal regions then evaluate the consistency of this signal with past experiences. If there is no prior experience, the realization of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\nInterestingly, ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is the conflict between false familiarity and rational knowledge, not just a feeling of familiarity. D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is a sign of well-functioning fac...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDDlwvvadzyKVndoXhxoMZcOe550pi7gnzekiIjCmkEV91fnr0RMJwXVwOg7hkrR8fRkOFpAuSKChgn4Ya6JzUK6XjhC+apxXpUqPQ94qFSjALq6MW0d2JsFyhOnNGIx0m4dZphgE",
            "title": "The Fascinating Science of D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu | Psychology Today",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/brain-reboot/202312/the-fascinating-science-of-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is the conflict between *false familiarity* and rational knowledge \u2014 not just a feeling of familiarity. It's actually considered a sign of well-functioning fact-checking mechanisms in the brain, not a memory error.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c Leading Theories\n\nScientists have proposed several mechanisms:\n\n1. **Memory Correction Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Akira O&#x27;Connor, a lecturer at the school of psychology and neuroscience at the University of St. Andrews in Scotland, presented research that sug...",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDDPCNEK/xmL+opH+cxoMuJj9uqVEZVCM+6sTIjCimx/xDnad6pCi0Y5Slvu8f+33Y2P+eIBfOqBhxTNzo3KM83vz/tKEE5ftx+hnFioqE12eox5hT/AEifHI/BSE6eKHN+AYBA==",
            "title": "What is the science behind d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu? | Live Science",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.livescience.com/health/psychology/what-is-the-science-behind-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Research by Akira O'Connor suggests that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by the brain *correcting* memory errors.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": " ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "As O\u2019Connor argues, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when the frontal regions of the brain attempt to correct an inaccurate memory. ",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDKrJaF+i7NwL+i4/yxoMsDhr/4xVjansTIngIjAEFWxDqj9UqAKl3wniEteYri4HW86iIL5ujIqP82cmsR488hYBMXiHm1s8rMB7c64qEyKceSMpZs+8dzfCsfdVQTsJmoEYBA==",
            "title": "Here's what d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu (or the lack of it) says about your brain health | BBC Science Focus Magazine",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.sciencefocus.com/the-human-body/deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Specifically, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when the frontal regions of the brain attempt to correct an inaccurate memory.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n2. **Familiarity Without Recall:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Scientists have a few different theories about how dual processing might play out: Familiarity and retrieving: The parts of your brain that recognize ...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDCopG8KxRb63rbxfixoM0SzUH2P+i1Yq50vqIjAF5D+BsvTql48JY2i/mHhAFBoQSn6j2NecMVl20HsLr97t+MIpkWSSqTQJebY71YwqFZ7LC96GH1DTheKWyjQ48j83XupWbRgE",
            "title": "Have You Experienced D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu? Causes and When to See a Doctor",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.webmd.com/mental-health/what-is-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "The parts of your brain that recognize familiar things and recall *why* they're familiar are supposed to work together. If you recognize something as familiar without the recall process kicking in to tell you where you recall it from, that may feel like d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n3. **Shortcut to Long-Term Memory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "When your brain absorbs information, it generally follows a specific path from short-term memory storage to long-term memory storage. The theory sugge...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDIzs0/N7uHTtaupKJxoMEVyWIeVAX7cnxIWlIjBR6ZVlVeACdFeVR1fJe+Pv+ACk4z6bC8V4Sj9JZKP9TGQj8hw0XkNH5o3IJcY7IUwqFb2+G6ajY2nOmUCPktErA/Pvi+KarBgE",
            "title": "What Causes D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu? Common Theories, Symptoms to Watch For, and More",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/mental-health/what-causes-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "When your brain absorbs information, it generally follows a specific path from short-term memory storage to long-term memory storage. The theory suggests that, sometimes, short-term memories can take a shortcut to long-term storage \u2014 making you feel as if you're retrieving a long-ago memory rather than something that happened in the last second.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n4. **Dual/Delayed Processing:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "You observe something, but the information you take in through your senses is transmitted to your brain along two separate routes. One of these routes...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDJzpI4P1AfGiFpiOPRoMZwKNlLx9hvZG3Om1IjDN2bznGW3+4lPe6PzS2rpdY9Vsam6JDBB5TnfuURgAJKlEyxZDSDduTSI2ATTphfoqFVAbhAtwciZiKF/wzoI2L4HrjveN3hgE",
            "title": "What Causes D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu? Common Theories, Symptoms to Watch For, and More",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/mental-health/what-causes-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "You observe something, but the information you take in through your senses is transmitted to your brain along two separate routes. One of these routes gets the information to your brain a little more rapidly than the other, and this delay \u2014 though extremely brief \u2014 leads your brain to read a single event as two different experiences.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n5. **Cryptomnesia:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Another possible explanation for the phenomenon of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is the occurrence of cryptomnesia, which is where information learned is forgotten but neve...",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDEruCKnRUbboUixROBoMJCeSgeuiSZgIg8hrIjCBMOBG/06JqwzCfyeiPmMiTC9ts4+mV3v/hipMlAMoKM0G5qTJ8hv2KGyzTrKJX6wqE/l/6oIJu6tsoNwPoXEc/OLQgz0YBA==",
            "title": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu - Wikipedia",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Another explanation is cryptomnesia, where information that was learned is forgotten but still stored in the brain, and similar occurrences invoke that contained knowledge, leading to a feeling of familiarity.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f What Triggers It?\n\n",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Fatigue and stress have been identified as potential contributors. When the brain is tired, its internal neuronal systems may struggle to regulate the...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDKAWmeMq97R+3FqyoxoMz4QxSWI3aVoHIqXxIjCFJoxBlo/QOYlOH9hwqpfMzWyPvfp1YstGyi8jvwCyTep2AHQkmwCmFv8wOjpirRgqFYA5IUYltKW2zmawWjh4mTdBd2KPUBgE",
            "title": "The Fascinating Science of D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu | Psychology Today",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/brain-reboot/202312/the-fascinating-science-of-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Fatigue and stress have been identified as potential contributors. When the brain is tired, its neuronal systems may struggle to regulate themselves, leading to a higher likelihood of misfirings and the sensation of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": " Dopaminergic drugs have also been linked to it \u2014 ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "\u201cThis could be why dopaminergic drugs, recreational or otherwise, tend to often cause elevated reports of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.\u201d ... There\u2019s also another factor. ",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDL7ANRQ9810X4BHbaBoMk+p790Kfnbel1KibIjDZac4e1SmI0fv77w+sH3MP/gdjN4HR5lOSfqkdoFVUgF4upsAkpKXmMTnGecQRWz8qE7EcXgPI1I4p7bWJQvsjq0vgWagYBA==",
            "title": "Here's what d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu (or the lack of it) says about your brain health | BBC Science Focus Magazine",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.sciencefocus.com/the-human-body/deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "dopaminergic drugs, recreational or otherwise, tend to often cause elevated reports of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcc9 Why Does It Decrease with Age?\n\n",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "\u201cIt\u2019s really interesting that younger people get more d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu \u2013 older people are normally expected to have more memory quirks. However, this happens a...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpIBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDJNO/NeafWCj7cJAyRoMWjmJmcE/3gExD8ZqIjDVWJCpmWMp2GN0xoZmypCGTIVQclwUDuKvqKoj9fPnwPJ1asmcU5tqj9NASGwEU3AqFriJOKgDX27cEyb4gTJsgUI2VJ4BmZsYBA==",
            "title": "Here's what d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu (or the lack of it) says about your brain health | BBC Science Focus Magazine",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.sciencefocus.com/the-human-body/deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Younger people get more d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu because they generally have a greater kind of excitatory activity in their brains \u2014 they're generally more active.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": " ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "\u201cUnfortunately, like many memory issues, it's just a natural part of ageing. You become less able to notice errors,\u201d says O\u2019Connor. ",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDJ8YQcIZubleM1X0JxoMmGaul+UbQx+OcuKXIjCWTQ9Ve4Tgfn3k0VQKgO1c8YWQ7l40CMhsR/hvtkcEFzKdYHoylnGx88P7yPHvPCIqFZRoYNB1XUdWWpW2i8LC/djI2Zr98BgE",
            "title": "Here's what d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu (or the lack of it) says about your brain health | BBC Science Focus Magazine",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.sciencefocus.com/the-human-body/deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Like many memory issues, decreased d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is \"just a natural part of ageing\" \u2014 you become less able to notice errors.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f When Is It a Concern?\n\n",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
